    text: "Friendships rarely end from conflict; they end from unconscious friction and structural drift. When a relationship lacks an active rupture, the decay is almost always logistical and psychological.\n\nProximity collapse: Most friendships are built on shared convenience (school, uni, work, shared local scenes). Once the shared container disappears, maintenance shifts from passive exposure to active scheduling.\nIdentity divergence: People evolve values, tastes, and coping mechanisms at different tempos. When the present-day overlap shrinks, the relationship survives only on nostalgia, which eventually runs dry.\nAsymmetric cognitive load: Career, partners, creative output, and routines consume baseline energy. Maintaining a connection that requires high coordination starts competing directly with recovery time.\nThe friction barrier: If catching up requires multiple rounds of calendar coordination rather than spontaneous drop-ins, the logistical friction quietly outpaces the perceived payoff.\nPassive drift rationalisation: Neither party wants to admit the dynamic has shifted, so both default to mutual silence, assuming the other is simply \"busy\" until the silence becomes permanent.\n\nWithout recurring, low-effort contact or evolving shared goals, a friendship defaults to an archive: mutually respected, but no longer operational.",
