    text: "In React, useEffect can appear to run twice in development because of React.StrictMode.\n\nThe important point is that React isn't simply \"calling your effect twice by accident.\" In development, Strict Mode deliberately performs an extra setup \u2192 cleanup \u2192 setup cycle to help detect effects that aren't written safely.\n\nExample\n\nuseEffect(() => {\nconsole.log(\"Effect ran\");\n\nreturn () => {\nconsole.log(\"Cleanup\");\n};\n}, []);\n\nWith Strict Mode, you may see: Effect ran, Cleanup, Effect ran \u2014 even though the dependency array is empty.\n\nConceptually, React does something like: Mount component \u2192 Run effect \u2192 Run cleanup \u2192 Simulate another mount \u2192 Run effect again\n\nThis happens only in development under Strict Mode. A production build doesn't intentionally perform this extra cycle.\n\nWhy does React do this?\n\nIt catches effects that have hidden assumptions about only running once.\n\nFor example, this is problematic:\n\nuseEffect(() => {\nconst connection = connectToServer();\n\n// forgot to disconnect!\n}, []);\n\nUnder Strict Mode you might see: connect, connect \u2014 which exposes the missing cleanup.\n\nThe correct version is:\n\nuseEffect(() => {\nconst connection = connectToServer();\n\nreturn () => {\nconnection.disconnect();\n};\n}, []);\n\nNow the development sequence becomes: connect, disconnect, connect \u2014 and the final state is exactly what you'd expect from one mounted component.\n\nA subtle but important distinction\n\nThere are actually two things people commonly describe as \"useEffect running twice\":\n\n1. The effect's setup runs twice\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n// setup\nreturn () => {\n// cleanup\n};\n}, []);\n\nStrict Mode does: setup \u2192 cleanup \u2192 setup\n\n2. The component itself renders twice\n\nStrict Mode can also invoke rendering-related code more than once in development to expose impure rendering.\n\nFor example:\n\nfunction App() {\nconsole.log(\"render\");\n\nuseEffect(() => {\nconsole.log(\"effect\");\n\nreturn () => console.log(\"cleanup\");\n}, []);\n\nreturn <div>Hello</div>;\n}\n\nYou can see output resembling: render, render, effect, cleanup, effect\n\nThe exact console behavior can vary depending on React version and DevTools, but the key idea is that Strict Mode intentionally stresses your component during development.\n\nThe mental model to use\n\nDon't write: \"My effect should only ever run once because I used [].\" Instead, write: \"[] means the effect doesn't react to dependency changes. It does not mean React guarantees a single setup call across the component's entire lifetime in development.\"\n\nAnd, most importantly: every effect should be safe to start, clean up, and start again. That's one of the core ideas behind React's effect model.",
