    text: "If it's happening most nights, I'd handle it in stages rather than immediately escalating.\n\nTalk to them once, calmly. Pick a daytime moment rather than confronting them while you're angry at 1 a.m. Say something like: \"Hey, I wanted to mention that I've been hearing quite a bit of noise from upstairs late at night, especially [music/footsteps/moving furniture]. It's been making it difficult to sleep. Would you mind keeping it down after about 10 p.m.?\"\n\nBe specific about the type of noise and times, and frame it around the impact on you rather than accusing them of being inconsiderate.\n\nGive them a chance to fix it. They may genuinely not realise how much sound carries between apartments. If they respond reasonably, you may find the problem disappears without needing anything formal.\n\nKeep a simple noise log if it continues. Record dates, approximate start/end times, type of noise, and how disruptive it was. Don't exaggerate\u2014just keep factual notes. This becomes useful if you eventually need to involve a landlord, building manager, owners corporation/body corporate, or another authority.\n\nIf talking doesn't work, put it in writing. A short, polite message creates a record: \"Just following up on our conversation about the late-night noise. Unfortunately it's still happening regularly, particularly between [times]. Could you please keep the noise down during those hours? I'd really appreciate it.\"\n\nEscalate if it's persistent or unreasonable. Depending on whether you're renting or owning, that could mean contacting your landlord/property manager, building manager, or owners corporation/body corporate. If the noise is extreme or involves something like threats or violence, that's a different situation and may warrant contacting the appropriate authorities.\n\nA couple of things I'd avoid: don't retaliate with banging on the ceiling, loud music, etc. It usually makes the relationship worse. Don't assume every sound is deliberate. Footsteps, children, plumbing, or ordinary apartment noise can be surprisingly loud through ceilings. Don't make the first conversation a threat about complaints or police unless there's a genuine safety issue.\n\nIf you're in Victoria, I can also explain the specific rules around residential noise, including what counts as unreasonable noise and what your options are if the neighbour won't cooperate.",
