    text: "The key question is how much the credit card is costing you in interest and whether the $5,000 is your only emergency cash.\n\nIn most cases, I'd prioritize it like this: keep a small emergency buffer, perhaps $1,000\u2013$2,000 if you have no other accessible savings. This prevents a car repair, medical bill, or unexpected expense from sending you straight back onto the card. Put the rest toward the credit-card balance. Credit-card interest is usually far higher than what you can reliably earn on savings or investments, so paying it down is effectively a high-return, low-risk use of your money. Stop adding new charges while paying it off, if possible. Once the card is cleared, rebuild your emergency fund to roughly 3\u20136 months of essential expenses. Then consider investing money you won't need for several years.\n\nExample\n\nIf you have $5,000 cash and $4,000 on a card: keep $1,500 as emergency savings, pay $3,500 toward the card. You'd have only $500 left on the card, rather than $4,000 accruing interest. Once it's gone, redirect the former card payments into your savings.\n\nOne important exception: if the credit card is on a genuine 0% interest promotion, the calculation changes. In that case, keeping more of the $5,000 in a high-interest savings account while making the required payments can make sense, provided you have a firm plan to clear the balance before the promotional period ends.\n\nIf you tell me (1) the card balance, (2) its interest rate, (3) your monthly essential expenses, and (4) whether the $5,000 is all the cash you have, I can work out exactly how I'd allocate the $5,000.",
