    text: "Yes\u2014for most people, learning to cook at home is worth it, even if you still order in regularly.\n\nThe key is not becoming someone who cooks elaborate meals every night. It's learning to make a handful of cheap, tasty, reliable meals.\n\nThe biggest benefits\n\nMoney: Home cooking is usually dramatically cheaper than restaurant delivery, especially once delivery fees and markups are included. Health: You control portions, oil, salt, protein, vegetables, etc. Convenience over time: Once you have 5\u201310 meals you can make almost automatically, cooking can take 15\u201330 minutes. Less decision fatigue: Having a few default meals means you're not deciding what to order every night. A useful life skill: You can feed yourself well almost anywhere and adapt meals to whatever ingredients you have. Better food: With a little practice, you can make certain things substantially better than mediocre takeaway.\n\nBut don't make it all-or-nothing\n\nI'd aim for something like: cook 3\u20135 times per week + order in 1\u20133 times.\n\nFor example, if you're exhausted after work, order in. If you have 30 minutes, cook. If you want something special, go to a restaurant. If you need cheap food for several days, cook a batch. If you don't feel like cooking, use leftovers or a frozen meal. If you want to learn, cook something new.\n\nThe mistake is thinking you need to go from \"I order everything\" to \"I cook every meal from scratch.\" That's unnecessary.\n\nA very good beginner strategy\n\nLearn these 5 categories rather than specific recipes: eggs (omelette, scrambled eggs, fried eggs), rice/noodle bowl (protein + rice/noodles + vegetables + sauce), pasta (tomato, pesto, cream-based, or meat sauce), roast/pan-cooked protein + vegetables (chicken, steak, fish, tofu), one-pot meal (curry, chilli, stir-fry, soup).\n\nOnce you can make those, you can improvise hundreds of meals.\n\nAnd there's a nice financial side effect: you don't need to stop ordering in. If learning to cook turns a $25\u201340 delivery habit into, say, three inexpensive home-cooked meals a week, you've gained a substantial amount of flexibility without making your life miserable.\n\nBottom line: Learn to cook, but treat it as a tool rather than a lifestyle. You want to reach the point where ordering food is something you choose, rather than something you need because you don't know what else to do.",
