    house: "openai",
    text: "There's no universal winner. In your late twenties, renting is often better if you value flexibility; buying is often better if you're financially stable and expect to stay put for several years.\n\nA useful rule of thumb\n\nBuying tends to make sense when: you expect to live in the same area for 5\u20137+ years, you have a solid emergency fund after paying the deposit and purchase costs, mortgage repayments are comfortably affordable\u2014not just barely serviceable, you have stable income and aren't expecting a major move, career change, or extended travel, you genuinely want the responsibilities of ownership.\n\nRenting tends to make sense when: you might move cities or countries within the next few years, buying would consume almost all your savings, you'd need to stretch your budget substantially to afford the mortgage, comparable homes are much cheaper to rent than to own, you have good opportunities to invest the money you don't put into a deposit.\n\nThe big mistake: comparing rent with the mortgage payment\n\nA $700/week rent versus a $700/week mortgage doesn't mean buying is cheaper. As an owner, you also pay things such as interest on the mortgage, property taxes/rates, insurance, maintenance and repairs, purchase and selling costs, potentially body-corporate/strata fees, and the opportunity cost of your deposit. On the other hand, part of a mortgage payment builds equity, whereas rent doesn't.\n\nYour late twenties aren't a special deadline\n\nThere's no financial requirement to own a home by 30. A financially comfortable 30-year-old renter with substantial investments can be in a much stronger position than a 30-year-old homeowner who is highly leveraged and house-poor.\n\nThe better question is: \"Given my expected time in the property, finances, and alternatives for my money, does buying beat renting?\"\n\nFor example, if you're 28 with $100k saved, earning a stable income, and expect to stay in the same city until your mid-thirties, I'd seriously investigate buying. If you're 28 and think you might relocate for work in two years, I'd lean toward renting and keeping your savings/investments liquid.\n\nIf you tell me your approximate income, savings, current rent, debts, and the price of the home you're considering (and your Australian city), I can run a realistic rent-vs-buy comparison for you.",
    source: "real",
