    text: "Here's the safe, standard way to jump-start a car using jumper cables.\n\nBefore you start\n\nMake sure both vehicles are off, in Park (or Neutral for a manual), with parking brakes on. Keep the cars close enough for the cables to reach, but don't let the vehicles touch. Turn off lights, radios, and other electrical accessories. Don't attempt this if the battery is cracked, leaking, badly swollen, or frozen.\n\nConnect the cables in this order\n\nRed \u2192 dead battery (+). Attach the red clamp to the positive + terminal of the dead battery.\n\nRed \u2192 good battery (+). Attach the other red clamp to the positive + terminal of the working battery.\n\nBlack \u2192 good battery (\u2212). Attach the black clamp to the negative \u2212 terminal of the working battery.\n\nBlack \u2192 dead car's metal ground. Attach the remaining black clamp to an unpainted metal part of the dead car's engine or chassis, away from the battery. This reduces the chance of sparks near the battery.\n\nStart the cars\n\nStart the working car and let it run for a minute or two. Try starting the dead car. Don't crank it continuously\u2014if it doesn't start after about 5\u201310 seconds, wait a little and try again. Once it starts, leave both cars running for several minutes.\n\nDisconnect in reverse order\n\nBlack clamp from the dead car's metal ground. Black clamp from the good battery's negative. Red clamp from the good battery's positive. Red clamp from the dead battery's positive. Don't let the clamps touch each other while they're connected.\n\nAfterward: Drive the revived car for roughly 20\u201330 minutes if possible, but understand that this may not fully recharge a severely discharged battery. If the car won't restart later, the battery may need charging or replacement\u2014or the charging system may have a problem.\n\nIf either car is a hybrid/EV, has a stop-start system, or has a battery in an unusual location, check its owner's manual first because the correct jump points can differ.",
