    text: "The difference is mostly about purpose, length, and country\u2014and the terms are sometimes used interchangeably.\n\nResume\n\nA resume is a concise, tailored summary of your professional experience. Usually 1\u20132 pages. Focuses on the experience and skills relevant to a particular job. Changes depending on the position you're applying for. Highlights achievements rather than documenting everything you've done. Common in the US and Canada. Think: \"Why am I a good fit for this job?\"\n\nCV (Curriculum Vitae)\n\nA CV is traditionally a more comprehensive record of your academic and professional career. Can be several pages long. Includes education, research, publications, presentations, awards, teaching, conferences, etc. Usually less tailored to a particular job. Commonly used for academic, research, medical, and some specialist positions. Think: \"What have I done throughout my career?\"\n\nThe confusing part: Australia and the UK\n\nIn Australia and the UK, people often use \"CV\" to mean essentially what Americans call a resume. If an Australian job advertisement asks for your CV, they generally aren't expecting a 10-page academic document\u2014they usually want a concise 2\u20133 page employment history.\n\nSo, practically: a resume is typically 1\u20132 pages, tailored to the job, doesn't usually include academic publications, and doesn't include a detailed career history; in Australian job applications it's often called a CV. A CV is typically 2+ pages, less tailored to the job, often includes academic publications, includes a detailed career history, and for Australian job applications it usually means a resume, though for academic/research applications it's usually the appropriate choice.\n\nRule of thumb: If you're applying for a normal corporate, retail, trades, government, or professional job in Australia, make a concise, tailored CV\u2014roughly 2 pages. If you're applying for an academic/research position, use a true academic CV with your publications, research, teaching, and credentials.\n\nIf you tell me what kind of job you're applying for, I can also show you what an Australian CV should actually look like in 2026.",
