    text: "If it's your first car, the choice between electric and petrol is less about which technology is \"better\" and more about how you actually use the car.\n\nPurchase price: EVs are often higher, although cheaper EVs are increasingly available. Petrol usually has more choice, especially used.\nFuel/energy cost: EVs are much cheaper, particularly with home charging. Petrol is more expensive and fluctuates with petrol prices.\nServicing: EVs are generally simpler and cheaper. Petrol requires more frequent servicing and has more mechanical parts.\nDriving: EVs are very quiet, with instant acceleration, easy in traffic. Petrol is familiar, responsive, with lots of choice.\nRefuelling: EVs are convenient if you can charge at home, slower on road trips. Petrol fills up in a few minutes almost anywhere.\nLong trips: EVs require some charging planning. Petrol is extremely straightforward.\nUsed-car market: EV is smaller, but growing. Petrol has a huge selection.\nRange anxiety: Can be a consideration on remote trips for EVs. Rarely an issue for petrol.\nEnvironmental impact: EVs are generally substantially lower over its lifecycle. Petrol has higher emissions.\nMechanical complexity: EVs are relatively low. Petrol is much higher.\n\n\u26a1 Why I'd consider an EV for a first car\n\nThe biggest advantage is running cost. The Australian Government estimates EVs can save up to 70% on fuel and around 40% on servicing, although your actual savings depend heavily on where/how you charge.\n\nThey're also exceptionally easy to drive. There's no traditional gearbox, no clutch, and regenerative braking can make stop-start traffic almost effortless.\n\nAnd range is less of an issue than it used to be. Most EVs sold in Australia now have roughly 250\u2013600 km of claimed range, while the average Australian driver travels only about 33 km per day.\n\nBut there's one big question: Can you charge at home?\n\nIf you have a garage/driveway and can plug in overnight, an EV becomes much more attractive. Around 80% of reported Australian EV charging happens at home.\n\n\u26fd Why petrol might still be smarter\n\nFor a first car, simplicity and flexibility are valuable.\n\nA petrol car makes more sense if: you live somewhere without convenient home charging, you regularly drive long distances or into remote areas, your budget is tight and you're buying used, you want the enormous choice available in the second-hand market, you don't want to think about charging stops on road trips.\n\nPetrol cars also have a mature repair ecosystem: virtually every mechanic knows how to work on them.\n\nOne thing I'd not do\n\nDon't assume an EV is automatically cheaper overall just because it's cheaper to run.\n\nSuppose you have a $25k petrol car, versus a $40k EV. The EV might save you hundreds or even over $1,000 per year in energy and maintenance, but it still has to recover that $15k purchase-price difference. The calculation changes dramatically if you're comparing similarly priced cars.\n\nThere are now Australian EVs below $30k, though, which makes that comparison considerably more interesting.\n\nMy rule of thumb\n\nI'd choose an EV if you can charge at home, most of your driving is local/commuting, and you plan to keep the car for several years.\n\nI'd choose petrol if you can't reliably charge at home, you do frequent long/remote trips, and you're primarily looking for an inexpensive used first car.\n\nAnd if you're buying new in Australia in 2026, I'd definitely include EVs in the shortlist rather than assuming they're a luxury purchase. Current Australian listings include EVs around the $24k\u2013$30k mark, alongside much more expensive models.\n\nIf you tell me your budget (e.g. $10k/$20k/$30k), how far you drive per week, and whether you have home charging, I can tell you which side I'd pick for your situation.",
