    text: "A habit tends to stick long-term when you stop relying on motivation and make the behavior increasingly automatic.\n\nThe biggest factors are:\n\nMake it ridiculously easy at first. Start with something small enough that you can do it even on a bad day. \"Read 2 pages\" is more sustainable than \"read for an hour every night.\"\n\nAttach it to an existing routine. Use a reliable cue: after I brush my teeth, I meditate for 2 minutes. Existing routines provide the reminder automatically.\n\nReduce friction. Make the desired action the easiest option. Put running shoes by the door, keep your guitar visible, prepare tomorrow's lunch tonight.\n\nMake the immediate experience rewarding. Long-term benefits are often too distant to motivate you. Give yourself an immediate payoff\u2014checking it off, listening to a favorite podcast while exercising, enjoying the feeling of completion.\n\nRepeat in a consistent context. Doing something at roughly the same time and place helps your brain associate that context with the behavior. Over time, the cue starts triggering the behavior with less conscious effort.\n\nDon't demand perfection. Missing once isn't the problem. Turning one missed day into \"I've fallen off the wagon\" is. A useful rule is never miss twice.\n\nIncrease gradually. Once the behavior feels normal, expand it. Two minutes of exercise can become ten; ten can become twenty. You're building the identity and routine before demanding performance.\n\nDesign around your real life. A habit that requires enormous willpower, perfect sleep, or an empty schedule probably won't survive a stressful month. The best habit is one that still works when life gets messy.\n\nThe basic formula\n\nCue \u2192 tiny behavior \u2192 immediate reward \u2192 repeat\n\nFor example: after morning coffee, write one sentence, tick it off, repeat tomorrow.\n\nEventually, the goal isn't to constantly convince yourself to do it. The cue simply starts the behavior.\n\nAnd there's an important distinction: habit formation isn't primarily about how many days you've done something. It depends heavily on how consistently the behavior occurs in a stable context and how easy/automatic it becomes. That's why a simple habit done reliably can stick better than an ambitious one pursued intensely for a few weeks.",
