    text: "Because friendships don't only end through conflict. Sometimes they simply stop being maintained by the circumstances that once made them easy.\n\nA few common reasons:\n\nLife changes. One person moves, changes jobs, starts a relationship, has children, or develops a different routine. You may still care about each other, but you no longer naturally cross paths.\n\nFriendship needs repetition. Closeness is partly built through small, frequent interactions. If those disappear, intimacy can gradually weaken without anyone deciding to withdraw.\n\nPeople change. Your interests, values, priorities, or personalities can shift. You might still like someone while having less and less to talk about.\n\nThe friendship becomes one-sided. Neither person necessarily does anything wrong, but both may assume the other will initiate. Eventually, \"we should catch up\" replaces actually catching up.\n\nNew relationships take up space. A new partner, new friends, or a demanding career can unintentionally displace older friendships.\n\nDistance changes the economics of friendship. Some friendships survive distance beautifully; others depended heavily on being physically nearby.\n\nThere isn't enough emotional urgency. With a close friend, you might make an effort to reconnect after six months. With a more peripheral friend, six months can quietly become two years.\n\nNothing is wrong\u2014but nothing is pulling you together anymore. This is perhaps the strangest kind. There's no betrayal or falling-out. The shared world you once inhabited simply disappears.\n\nAnd importantly, fading doesn't necessarily mean the friendship was fake or unimportant. A friendship can be deeply meaningful for a particular period of your life without lasting indefinitely.\n\nSometimes two people can meet again years later and immediately recover the old warmth. Other times, you realize that what you miss isn't necessarily the person as they are now\u2014it's who you both were when the friendship was alive.\n\nThat's one of the bittersweet things about friendship: some relationships end because something went wrong; others end because time quietly changes the shape of both people's lives.",
